#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105543 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e9867f-15ba-5073-a20f-542045fced08" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalue that Corresponds to a Given Eigenvector"}] :question/id #uuid "61bb0d60-a61e-5485-9df5-308e16dfd1aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-5476" :question/problem "Determine if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/82b99a6e1a271ac0b205a3199b023a582877692c3a61dd4b7144312342fea761.png) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/2b9e07d0a0089107dc34bb092167719b6e83a4c41ee9f055949a43549449d65e.png) If so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v}=4 \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105544 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/id #uuid "f4ff15d3-1a6e-56c0-a7c4-13f18ef1a8e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5477" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/f35353367a395e7b3560aa6f7ef0da0936cefbced9e76d6d7d8f91426a7a1c9b.png) is an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/0b4f33107845bf52d80802844bc6faaec6ab9f4bd159a87838497d2f6abe72d1.png) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2.$\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/ea0bc4d487e57434694dac1835e082f63c9467b496933c8e36770334e77713f5.png) is an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/c87d5c13bbd73d56f44af3ec1b4a867c86e8bf4f709fa869ab8d142424cbe8c4.png) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 5,$ then $2\\mathbf{v}$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 5.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v}=2 \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. An eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector.\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(2v)=2Av=2(5v)=5(2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(2\\mathbf{v})=5( 2 \\mathbf{v} ),$ and $2 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ this implies that $2 \\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda = 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\"\n\n**Note:** Statement III is true in the general case, too. In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105545 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f5608f-a9f5-5e6a-b308-540ed8b29259" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Given an Eigenvalue and Eigenvector of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "6cba45d6-5a69-5b83-937c-c19368aaeea9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5478" :question/problem "An eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/f5ac973d5d7dd390f8cf9231515815f334d0c01dafeab14ba58fef25db4a8e8d.png) corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda=6c$ is  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/a7e303f19be04db829faaa41133c70d7b6e5647ed75063674ca564a2952c09eb.png) Determine the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] & =6c[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2+2c \\\\ 1+4c\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6c \\\\ 12{c}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2+2c=6c \\\\ 1+4c=12{c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2+2c=6c \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $c=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+4c & =12{c}^{2} \\\\ 1+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} & =12{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ 3 & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solutions of the system is $c=\\dfrac{1}{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105551 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49c501da-161c-53be-8349-571cc6a07975" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting the Product of a Matrix and an Eigenvector Given its Corresponding Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "df40f37e-0d8f-5eb2-ad8a-9257440851df" :question/math-academy-id "e-5891" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/fdfa0528b5476ed0246679c45fd31824470063a68f33c4c9af0f714ad201538f.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $2.$ Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}.$" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/9f9c7bc79c4e294e45f34d9e10c0e429c33ce95730d13aaaf337f7a9cb22f1a3.png) corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av=2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/78e1e3c015adad2c826a64e255bd982941dc9fe50679acc2bae3f00b31bd9f39.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e9867f-15ba-5073-a20f-542045fced08" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalue that Corresponds to a Given Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2eb3398-ae19-5ca2-ab18-d8935136c5e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333546} {:answer/id #uuid "3a0b06b3-08a2-5110-ad50-78ebb74c0b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333547} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9a45ec-a2d4-5173-9908-784eaabd280e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333548} {:answer/id #uuid "a60a30d1-8ebf-5d01-9942-d439f4339c66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333549} {:answer/id #uuid "57a94b9a-0482-5936-b6e8-ad04da8947d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2eb3398-ae19-5ca2-ab18-d8935136c5e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333546} :answer-field/id #uuid "34016eca-7fa9-5357-b97a-23860f2ff5c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b22af89b-1092-506b-925c-dc472e0f7d9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106744" :question/problem "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1=\\lambda  \\\\ -2=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e9867f-15ba-5073-a20f-542045fced08" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalue that Corresponds to a Given Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aeb963b-fbfa-56f5-89f9-e052c82bed07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371183} {:answer/id #uuid "4b15870c-a2de-50b5-8309-f8086e971e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371184} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a446d5-95ea-5fde-84d1-ee7b75b562c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371185} {:answer/id #uuid "90bcdddc-cfaf-56fd-ba01-40fdf4797d42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371186} {:answer/id #uuid "6a124d98-9f14-5eeb-9563-c3b4fae1d486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b15870c-a2de-50b5-8309-f8086e971e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371184} :answer-field/id #uuid "12f19e8f-2221-5360-bf0d-5edb74f3630d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56a4f3a9-ccca-53d2-8026-09ec5330fdbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-106775" :question/problem "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]=\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}16=2\\lambda  \\\\ -8=-4\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49c501da-161c-53be-8349-571cc6a07975" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting the Product of a Matrix and an Eigenvector Given its Corresponding Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc4c69f1-cb9a-5bcc-be03-c2be1f367115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/441a9f0ee158ae2ebdddb223463887ca9f2d5e84a6fc925c80b0aa9ccf4bc65c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371190} {:answer/id #uuid "449b6556-2c76-5e8a-9fec-c0a79a7dcb69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/addd53cc78d53e0bb3ee5f5d51128afef8723186516db3aa092dcb97086d616f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371191} {:answer/id #uuid "2b2f40e5-cf55-5b08-869a-89b2fd80908d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/b595dff95f29976442a7a8fd0acf3baf2f518d364fa55b8e4ffb06e72745260f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371192} {:answer/id #uuid "0a23d2d5-4039-556e-8103-c5bba1eb25e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/eb014dbb24f2f087c558d40d2ddb198f225c1ad50a66e6cf5daaaef7eec33021.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371193} {:answer/id #uuid "6c86dc1f-e219-51bd-baea-c914557a1575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/99e3776bfa4048703042970a2479a86c749e3e2a547dce966fceb23b671d66f2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c86dc1f-e219-51bd-baea-c914557a1575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/99e3776bfa4048703042970a2479a86c749e3e2a547dce966fceb23b671d66f2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371194} :answer-field/id #uuid "533f3231-9b54-5ec9-a1e4-399f80aa7dda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8bb61574-69ec-5f86-9b0c-3dbbf6ec3612" :question/math-academy-id "q-106890" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/4adc01d460f42f134f2e6c8da1e2fe516c8e6cfb64bcfeb7a5db89cf93f1fc11.png)\n\nThe vector $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $C$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $-4.$ Find the vector $Cv.$" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cv & =-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/99e3776bfa4048703042970a2479a86c749e3e2a547dce966fceb23b671d66f2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49c501da-161c-53be-8349-571cc6a07975" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting the Product of a Matrix and an Eigenvector Given its Corresponding Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "829da8d6-9786-5d11-a2bb-62bca48ed9e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/0b7e76c47db52dff6dd985ef03fe1233f533249fb200fb63e4a0390cf98b7e34.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371197} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6f9